unit(911,3,'Zero-dimensional and absolutely flat rings',[43],'460–509','« ne pas confondre » (affine and presheaf coproducts)')
unit(912,3,'Characteristic, pro-objects and filtered intersections of open subtopoi',[44,45,46,47],'511–731','« Pro(R) »')
unit(913,3,'Gluing characteristic theories and decompositions by connected components',[48,49,50],'733–860','« à vérifier »; « construction à préciser »')

addclaim(38,910,3,[37,39],'6–136;248–267','Paraphrase: the lattice of ring conditions and the ring/local/strictly-local classifiers.','Internal commutative unital rings are classified by PSh((CRing_fp)^op); over Z, finite type is finite presentation. The Zariski and étale topologies give nonzero local and strictly henselian local rings respectively. Ambiguous arrows in the opening property lattice are not assertions.','classifier identifications stated; lattice includes canceled and unoriented edges','Finite-limit models of (CRing_fp)^op are rings. Covering by D(f),D(1−f) gives the local axiom; the empty cover excludes the zero ring. Étale lifting gives strict henselianity. The elementary implications field⇒domain/local and reduced+irreducible nonempty⇒domain are checked, but connected does not imply local (Z), and reduced does not imply domain (k×k).','SGA4 IV1.2 supplies the classifier mechanism. Gabber–Kelly Theorems2.3 and2.6/Table1 give exact Zariski/Nisnevich/étale local-ring descriptions under a separated noetherian base.',['SGA4I1972','GabberKelly2015'],'A')
addclaim(39,910,3,[37,42,43],'28–136;414–458;462–486','Paraphrase: compact spectra, stationary powers and unit/nilpotent decomposition.','For a commutative ring A, Spec(A) is zero-dimensional iff each f admits an idempotent decomposition A≃A_f×A/f^nA for some n, iff the principal ideals f^nA eventually stabilize. Compact here means Hausdorff compact, not merely quasi-compact.','proof sketched; the artinian-testing comparison is wholly canceled','If Spec(A) is zero-dimensional, D(f) is clopen and equals D(e) for an idempotent e; on eA, f is a unit, and on(1−e)A it is nilpotent. Conversely the decomposition forces every prime quotient to be a field. Stationary powers give f^n=f^{n+1}g and hence the same unit/nilpotent separation. Testing only artinian local rings is not established by this argument.','Stevenson §3 verifies the reduced zero-dimensional/absolutely-flat case; the nilpotent extension and stabilization criterion are proved here. Schröer’s nonnoetherian local examples show why no artinian-testing reduction is silently assumed.',['Stevenson2014','Schroer2016'])
addclaim(40,910,3,[39],'268–275','Paraphrase: are fppf coverings detected on noetherian ultralocal rings?','If ultralocal means fppf-local as in the displayed lifting condition, noetherian test rings do not detect fppf coverings. The correct conservative family must allow nonnoetherian fppf-local rings.','open on the pages; false noetherian-testing proposal','Schröer Corollary4.5 shows these noetherian rings are exactly algebraically closed fields: p=p² and Nakayama kill the maximal ideal. Spec(F_p)→Spec(F_p[ε]/ε²) lifts every algebraically closed field point but is not an fppf covering sieve: any faithfully flat extension is injective and cannot kill ε. The finite dual-number computation is saved.','Schröer, Points in the fppf topology, arXiv2014/final2016, Corollary4.5 and Theorem5.5; §6 constructs points. The exact fppf interpretation is retained because the manuscript’s ultralocal term is not independently defined elsewhere here.',['Schroer2016'],'C',event='refuted, with refined statement RF-SA23-groupes-040')
addclaim(41,910,3,[39,43],'253–275;497–509','Paraphrase: test rings for henselian, étale, fppf and algebraically/separably closed-field topologies.','Over a separated noetherian base, the topology-local affine test objects identify local rings for Zariski, henselian local rings for Nisnevich, and strictly henselian local rings for étale. Integral-domain/AIC and valuation descriptions depend on the precise topology; fppf needs a separate theorem.','stated as a collection of definitions and questions','Gabber–Kelly’s Table1 matches each named topology and its affine local objects, with finite-refinable covering hypotheses. A separably closed field is strictly henselian, while an algebraically closed field is fppf-local. The fppf table entry is not inferred from Gabber–Kelly, which omits it; Schröer supplies it separately.','Gabber–Kelly arXiv1407.5782 (2014), Theorems2.3,2.6 and Table1; Schröer Corollary4.5 and §§5–6. The noetherian source/base condition does not say the local test rings are noetherian.',['GabberKelly2015','Schroer2016'])
addclaim(42,910,3,[39],'277–306','Paraphrase: reduced rings form a finite-limit theory with a presheaf classifier.','Reduced commutative rings are models of the cartesian sequents x^n=0⇒x=0 (n≥1); their finitely presentable theory objects are reductions of finitely presented rings, and the classifier is the corresponding presheaf topos.','proof sketched','Reduced rings are a reflective full subcategory of rings with reflector A↦A_red and are closed under filtered colimits and all limits. The category is locally finitely presentable; maps from A_red to reduced B are maps A→B. Its finite colimits are reduced ring colimits. Power-map kernels in the source are subsets, not additive kernels; their union is the nilradical.','Makkai–Pitts Theorem2.4 gives the limits/filtered-colimit closure criterion; the explicit reduction adjunction proves the finite-presentation and classifier identification.',['MakkaiPitts1987','SGA4I1972'])
addclaim(43,910,3,[39,40],'308–357','Paraphrase: integral rings and irreducible rings are imposed by axes covering zero-product loci.','Domains require0≠1 and ab=0⇒a=0∨b=0. Irreducible rings require proper prime nilradical, equivalently A_red a domain; their geometric axiom uses an unbounded disjunction over nilpotence exponents. Reduced irreducible components impose domains, not arbitrary irreducible rings.','formulas stated; competing component readings in the transcription','The two axes jointly cover the zero-product locus exactly for a domain. For irreducibility, (ab)^n=0 implies locally a^m=0 or b^m=0 for some m. A finite cover by reduced irreducible components has the ordinary overlap equalizer sheaf condition; it does not make the components disjoint. The source’s reduced/induced component alternative changes the theory.','Gabber–Kelly Table1 identifies integral domains with closed-topology local objects. The nilradical-prime formulation is checked directly; the exact alternative topology for nonreduced irreducible rings is left sensitive to the recorded reading.',['GabberKelly2015'],'C')
addclaim(44,910,3,[40],'355–365','Paraphrase: the irreducible-ring quotient is called coherent after a deletion, while its topology is said not quasi-compact.','In the given ring signature, irreducibility of Spec is not axiomatizable by a coherent first-order theory. This does not by itself decide whether its classifying topos has some different coherent presentation.','competing canceled/inserted statements; no complete proof','For n≥2 let A_n=k[x,y]/(xy,x^n,y^n), each with one prime ideal. In a nonprincipal ultraproduct, x and y are nonnilpotent because every fixed power survives eventually, yet xy=0. Its nilradical is not prime. First-order coherent axioms are preserved by ultraproducts, so no such axiomatization in this signature exists. Failure of one site to be coherent is not an intrinsic topos invariant.','The geometric/coherent distinction is explicit in Beke’s opening definitions. Searches found no exact theorem deciding the intrinsic coherence of the manuscript’s ambiguously specified quotient; the rigorous signature-level counterexample is the result established here.',['BekePresheafType'],'C',confidence='medium',event='refuted, with refined statement RF-SA23-groupes-044')
addclaim(45,910,3,[40,41],'367–410','Paraphrase: connected-ring classifiers and finite-sum sheaf conditions.','Connected spectrum is the idempotent axiom e²=e⇒e=0∨e=1; nonempty connected spectrum additionally excludes the zero ring. On finite-type affine schemes, the connected-component topology yields the presheaf description on the connected pieces, with the empty-object convention made explicit.','proof sketched; the empty convention is changed in the text','Finite-type affines have finitely many connected components. A sheaf is determined by its values on those components and takes their coproduct to a product. Preserving nonempty finite coproducts leaves the empty value unconstrained; preserving the empty coproduct forces it to1. The source’s two conventions must not be merged.','The small-site comparison and open/closed quotient mechanism are SGA4 IV1.2 and9.2–9.3. The component decomposition gives the exact elementary proof for this site.',['SGA4I1972'],'A')
addclaim(46,911,3,[43],'462–486','Paraphrase: D_n is the monomorphic unit/nilpotent decomposition object.','The equations e²=e, f^n(1−e)=0, g(1−e)=0, fg=e define D_n→A¹ as a monomorphism. As an affine scheme, D_n≃G_m⊔Spec(Z[t]/t^n); this is not the coproduct of the represented presheaves.','proved there with an explicit warning about coproducts','The idempotent is uniquely the component D(f); g is f inverse on that component and0 elsewhere. Decomposing by e=1 and e=0 gives the displayed affine coproduct. A section over a disconnected test affine may use both components, so it need not choose one presheaf summand. Finite ring checks are saved.','The absolute-flat/quasi-inverse literature (Olivier Proposition5 and Caramello–Johnstone §1) matches n=1; the general nilpotent equation calculation is independently checked.',['Olivier1967','CaramelloJohnstone2009'])
addclaim(47,911,3,[43],'466–480','Paraphrase: for every U, A(U) has compact spectrum.','The geometric internal unit-or-nilpotent condition permits locally varying exponents. It is weaker than demanding every ordinary section ring A(U) be zero-dimensional.','false under the literal global-section reading','In E=Set^N let A_n=k[ε]/ε^{n+1}. Each component satisfies the geometric condition, hence the internal ring does. Global sections are the product, and f=(ε_n) is not nilpotent. On any nonzero idempotent component it is never a unit, so no global unit/nilpotent decomposition exists.','The local meaning of geometric disjunction follows from the sheaf/topos semantics in SGA4. The explicit product example refutes only the global-section reading and does not alter the transcription.',['SGA4I1972','CaramelloJohnstone2009'],'C',event='refuted, with refined statement RF-SA23-groupes-047')
addclaim(48,911,3,[43],'487–509','Paraphrase: reduced compact-spectrum rings, pseudofields and the constructible reflection.','Reduced zero-dimensional commutative rings are absolutely flat and have unique quasi-inverses a*, a²a*=a and a(a*)²=a*. The universal reflection A→A_abs is obtained by adjoining these elements and induces a bijection on spectra with identical residue fields.','proof sketched; one displayed equation has a sign/factor error','Uniqueness follows by comparing two quasi-inverses and using reducedness. For f=2 in Q, the source’s e(1−f)=0 fails with e=1; the needed relation is f(1−e)=0. Olivier’s construction A[x_a]/(a²x_a−a,ax_a²−x_a) gives the universal reflection, and its spectrum has the constructible topology.','Olivier, seminar talks20Dec1967/3Jan1968, Proposition5; Stevenson arXiv2012/journal2014, Proposition3.3 and Theorem3.9; Caramello–Johnstone §1 proves uniqueness. This is an exact match to the identifiable reflection, not a license to identify every proposed coverage.',['Olivier1967','Stevenson2014','CaramelloJohnstone2009'])
addclaim(49,912,3,[44,45],'513–614','Paraphrase: fixed characteristic, inversion of integers and characteristic zero.','n·1=0 and n invertible are cartesian ring quotients represented by subterminal objects of the affine syntactic category and hence open subtopoi. Requiring every positive integer invertible gives Q-algebras, not all rings of characteristic0.','proof sketched; characteristic-zero terminology ambiguous','For n=1 the first quotient permits only the zero ring, whose theory has classifier Set rather than the degenerate topos. Z has characteristic0 but is not a Q-algebra. A Λ-algebra structure is uniquely determined by the underlying ring only when Z→Λ is an epimorphism in commutative rings; arbitrary Λ does not have this property. Over nonnoetherian Λ use finitely presented, not merely finite-type, algebras.','SGA4 IV9.2 gives the open-quotient criterion. Olivier’s ring-epimorphism framework supplies the relevant meaning of uniqueness. Each elementary ring assertion is separately checked here.',['SGA4I1972','Olivier1967'],'C')
addclaim(50,912,3,[45,46],'606–630','Paraphrase: Pro(R) embeds in PSh(R) via limits of representables.','The naive limit-of-representables functor Pro(R)→PSh(R) need not be fully faithful. Pro-objects are fully faithfully represented by covariant left-exact functors with the opposite convention, not by this unrestricted presheaf limit.','false as stated','For R=FinSet, a profinite set P maps to the presheaf T↦Map(T,underlying P), since finite discrete test sets impose no continuity. The indicator of the all-zero sequence {0,1}^N→{0,1} is a natural transformation of these presheaves but is discontinuous, hence not a pro-morphism.','The correct Ind/Pro universal-property framework is in Kelly and Lack–Power. The discontinuous-map example completely checks the claimed full-faithfulness failure.',['Kelly1982','LackPower2009'],'C',event='refuted, with refined statement RF-SA23-groupes-050')
addclaim(51,912,3,[45,46,48],'597–670;738–755','Paraphrase: the rational locus might be the open of an inverse limit; later it is explicitly said not open.','An infinite intersection of open classifying subtopoi need not be open. In the ring classifier the Q-algebra subtopos is nontrivial and not the open represented by lim_n Spec Z[1/n].','abandoned extension attempt; corrected by himself at scan48:753','A nonzero finitely generated Z-algebra has a maximal ideal with finite-characteristic residue field (Stacks00GB), so it cannot contain Q. Thus on finite-type affine Z-tests the inverse-limit presheaf is the same as the representable empty affine, although Q is a model of the Q-algebra theory. This identifies the failed presheaf-limit step.','SGA IV9.2.6 states preservation of finite intersections, not arbitrary intersections. Stacks00GB supplies the exact finite-type Jacobson fact; C050 identifies the general pro-embedding error.',['SGA4I1972','StacksJacobson'],'C')
addclaim(52,912,3,[38,46],'140–205;618–670','Paraphrase: slices R/S, the three open-embedding functors, and filtered localization of the slices.','For a mono S→1 in small finitely complete R, PSh(R/S)≃PSh(R)/yS; i_! is forgetful, i* is product with S, and i_* is internal Hom(S,-). Inverting a small filtered family Sα→1 uses the finite-limit localization colim R/Sα, not the ambient presheaf inverse limit.','proof sketched; later warning distinguishes i_! from i_*','The slice equivalence follows by the category of elements of yS. The adjunctions follow from product/exponential universal properties. Exact functors on the filtered localization are exactly lex functors making every Sα terminal, giving the classifier. For an arbitrary subterminal presheaf use its sieve of objects rather than R/S if it is not representable.','SGA IV9.2.1–9.2.6, especially Remark9.2.3 printed451–452, explicitly warns against confusing the two full embeddings. These exact pages were opened in the retypeset primary text.',['SGA4I1972'],'A')
addclaim(53,912,3,[47],'674–731','Paraphrase: perfect rings and a characteristic-zero reduced convention.','In characteristic p, requiring Frobenius to be invertible defines the theory of perfect rings and implies reducedness. Its finitely presentable objects are perfections of finitely presented F_p-algebras in the perfect-ring category. The characteristic0 margin proposes a convention, not a standard theorem.','construction stated; characteristic0 margin queried','Frobenius injective kills any nilpotent after enough p-powers. Adjoining inverse Frobenius is an algebraic operation with equations, and perfection is the filtered colimit along Frobenius; the adjunction transports finite presentations. Adding quasi-inverses gives perfect absolutely flat rings; adding nontriviality/locality gives perfect fields.','The algebraic-theory presentation mechanism is established in Adámek–Lawvere–Rosický; the explicit perfection argument is re-proved. Searches did not identify a standard char0 definition matching the marginal question.',['AdamekLawvereRosicky2003','CaramelloJohnstone2009'])
addclaim(54,913,3,[48,49],'771–815','Paraphrase: local factorization through V or W, the largest factorization opens, and gluing should give a subtopos.','If the factorization loci are open and stable under geometric inverse image, and the specified open pieces glue along their open overlap, the resulting map to B is an inclusion by local full faithfulness. Existence of such stable largest opens for arbitrary subtopoi is not automatic.','open steps; the overlap identity is answered on the page; construction still marked to specify','When largest opens exist, the one for V∩W is their intersection by its universal property, as the text itself notes. On an open cover, maps and natural transformations glue; full faithfulness on each included piece gives full faithfulness globally, hence the SGA9.1.6 inclusion criterion. This does not prove the missing stability/existence premise for arbitrary V,W.','SGA IV9.1.6 and9.2.5 settle the inclusion and open base-change tools. Theorem9.5.4 proves a different precise open/closed recollement theorem; it must not be substituted for the source’s arbitrary two-subtopos criterion. Searches did not locate the unqualified stable-factorization-locus theorem.',['SGA4I1972'],'C')
addclaim(55,913,3,[48,49,50],'729–760;816–830','Paraphrase: prime-characteristic and rational theories can be glued; the later page says OK and gives the amalgamated category.','Rings whose base is locally partitioned into prime-field algebra structures are classified by the presheaf category on finite-type affine schemes over the prime fields, with their empty affine objects identified. Components excluding the zero ring are disjoint; those allowing it meet in the common open Set point.','initial infinite-intersection attempt canceled; specific site construction accepted later','Different prime characteristics have only the zero ring in common. The opposite affine categories therefore amalgamate at the empty affine; flat functors give the corresponding local choices of a prime-field component. Open gluing of the pieces gives the topos and its point criterion: the glued topos has enough points iff each open piece does. This is local constancy of the prime-field choice in the base topos, not a claim that every ring has such a decomposition.','SGA IV9.2 and the site/flat-functor theorem give the checked gluing mechanism. The source’s explicit small amalgamated category allows a direct proof even though the general criterion C054 remains conditional.',['SGA4I1972'],'A')
addclaim(56,913,3,[50],'832–860','Paraphrase: at most n connected components should be a geometric condition.','For ordinary rings, at most n connected components is equivalent to a Boolean idempotent algebra with at most n atoms, and is expressed by the coherent bound that any n+1 orthogonal idempotents summing1 contain0. Its equivalence to the source’s local decomposition in every topos needs a constructive argument.','definition and construction sketched; base-change compatibility queried on next leaf','The Boolean bound makes the Boolean algebra finite, hence decomposes1 into at most n atoms and A into connected factors. Conversely a product of at most n connected rings has exactly that idempotent bound. This Set-level proof does not construct stable largest connected-factor loci internally.','The geometric axiom/classifier construction is SGA IV9.1; the finite Boolean calculation is proved here. No searched primary source was found proving the exact unqualified internal-largest-locus construction.',['SGA4I1972'],'C')
addclaim(57,913,4,[51],'4–16','Paraphrase: the choices of a decomposition into n connected nonzero factors form an S_n-pseudotorsor; do pseudotorsors commute with base change?','For a fixed ring with n connected nonzero factors, ordered decompositions form an S_n-torsor. An already given G-pseudotorsor, defined by G×P≃P×P, is preserved by geometric inverse image; adding P→1 epi gives a torsor.','lemma and base-change question explicitly flagged','Atoms of the Boolean idempotent algebra determine the decomposition, uniquely up to permutation. The pseudotorsor defining isomorphism is a finite-limit identity and is preserved by f*; epimorphic local inhabitance is also preserved. This answers the property-level question but not whether the assignment of all decompositions is geometric.','SGA IV1.2 and the classifying topos B_G identify torsors; preservation of the displayed defining equation is a direct check.',['SGA4I1972'],'A')
addclaim(58,913,4,[51],'4–16','Paraphrase: exact rank n and the torsor construction should give a modular topos for the full ring property.','For n≥2, the full category of rings with exactly n connected components, with all ring homomorphisms, is not a geometric full subtheory. A theory retaining decomposition/torsor data has different morphisms.','construction proposed; compatibility left open','Take A=k^n and repeat the idempotent endomorphism (a1,…,an)↦(a1,…,a1). Each stage has n connected components, while the filtered colimit is its image k and has one. A geometric full subtheory must be closed under these filtered colimits. The finite k example and idempotent-image calculation are saved.','The SGA geometric-model closure test settles the full-property interpretation negatively. It does not refute a moduli theory with specified decomposition and equivariant morphisms.',['SGA4I1972'],'C',event='refuted, with refined statement RF-SA23-groupes-058')

addproblem(19,'Ultralocal noetherian point testing',[occurrence(3,[39],'268–275')],'Do noetherian ultralocal rings detect fppf coverings, and can local rings embed into ind-faithfully-flat ultralocal ones?','The noetherian detection claim is false; conservative tests require nonnoetherian fppf-local rings. The proposed construction must retain locality and faithful-flatness hypotheses.','C040 and Schröer Corollary4.5 give the exact obstruction; his Theorem5.5 constructs fppf-local limit rings and §6 points.',['Schroer2016'],worth='The negative answer is settled; a precise comparison of the proposed construction with Schröer’s transfinite one is a useful commentary task.')
addproblem(20,'Irreducible rings and the coherent reading',[occurrence(3,[40],'355–365')],'Is the irreducible-ring quotient coherent, and which scheme structure is used on components?','The ring-signature axiom is not coherent; intrinsic topos coherence requires a separate argument and the correct component reading.','C043–044 gives the exact distinction and ultraproduct counterexample; Gabber–Kelly only settles the reduced integral-domain topology.',['GabberKelly2015','BekePresheafType'],worth='High-value reading and theorem-identification task; inspect the ink on induced/reduced and coherent before choosing a stronger intrinsic claim.')
addproblem(21,'Connected and zero-dimensional conventions',[occurrence(3,[40,41],'367–410'),occurrence(3,[42],'414–458','comparison paragraph445–458 wholly canceled')],'Do the component-site formulas include the empty ring, and do artinian tests suffice for compact spectra?','Connected and nonempty connected theories differ by the empty-cover axiom. The zero-dimensional condition is geometric internally; artinian-only testing is not proved by the canceled text.','C039,C045–047 state the valid formulas and the global-section counterexample.',['SGA4I1972','Stevenson2014','Schroer2016'])
addproblem(22,'Universal unit/nilpotent decomposition and absolute-flat reflection',[occurrence(3,[43],'462–509')],'Do the D_n equations and constructible reflection give the stated classifier?','The equations and the unique quasi-inverse reflection are valid after the recorded factor correction; geometric and global formulations differ.','Olivier Proposition5, Stevenson3.9 and Caramello–Johnstone§1 exactly match C046–048.',['Olivier1967','Stevenson2014','CaramelloJohnstone2009'])
addproblem(23,'Characteristic zero and the pro-object shortcut',[occurrence(3,[45,46],'597–670'),occurrence(3,[48],'738–755','earlier attempt corrected by the non-open statement')],'Can the intersection of rational-localization opens be represented by a pro-object presheaf open?','No in general; use the finite-limit localization of the syntactic category, and distinguish Q-algebras from all characteristic-zero rings.','C049–052 gives the discontinuous-profinite-map counterexample, the finite-type-Z obstruction and the exact slice construction.',['SGA4I1972','StacksJacobson','Kelly1982'])
addproblem(24,'Perfect rings in characteristic zero',[occurrence(3,[47],'674–731')],'Should perfect in characteristic0 mean reduced?','The positive-characteristic Frobenius theory is precise; the char0 phrase is an optional convention not a consequence.','C053 proves the Frobenius/perfection claims and records the searched identification limit.',['AdamekLawvereRosicky2003','CaramelloJohnstone2009'])
addproblem(25,'Local factorization through two subtopoi',[occurrence(3,[48,49],'771–815')],'Find stable largest factorization opens, prove the glued morphism is an inclusion, and prove the sufficiency criterion.','Open pieces with geometric stable factorization data glue; the unqualified largest-locus premise remains unproved.','C054 uses the exact SGA9.1.6 criterion and9.2.5 base change. SGA9.5.4 open/closed recollement is a different theorem, explicitly separated.',['SGA4I1972'],worth='Worth a focused run: first translate the displayed condition into local operators and test whether the factorization locus is preserved by every inverse image.')
addproblem(26,'Characteristic gluing and points',[occurrence(3,[47,48],'729–760','initial infinite-intersection formulation canceled'),occurrence(3,[49,50],'816–830','specific site construction answered OK')],'Glue the positive-prime and rational theories and determine enough points.','Use the small prime-field affine categories with their empty objects identified, keeping zero-ring and nonzero-ring variants separate.','C055 supplies the explicit classifier and open-cover conservativity proof using SGAIV1.2/9.2.',['SGA4I1972'])
addproblem(27,'Connected-component rank and decomposition torsors',[occurrence(3,[50],'832–860'),occurrence(4,[51],'4–16')],'Do the largest connected loci and the sheaf of decompositions commute with base change; are pseudotorsors stable?','Pseudotorsors themselves are stable. Exact rank n with all ring maps fails filtered-colimit closure, whereas a specified decomposition theory is a different moduli problem.','C056–058 distinguishes the Set-level Boolean bound, torsor uniqueness and the explicit k^n collapse counterexample.',['SGA4I1972'],worth='A constructive analysis of rank≤n with explicit decomposition data is worthwhile; the exact-rank full-property claim is already refuted.')

